<h2>What Is Digital Citizenship?</h2>
<p>
  A <span class="keyword" data-def="Digital citizenship means using technology responsibly, safely, and respectfully.">digital citizen</span>
  is someone who uses the internet and digital tools in a safe, respectful, and responsible way.
  Your choices online affect you and the people around you, even when you are not face-to-face.
</p>

<h3>Key idea</h3>
<p>
  Your online world is part of your real world. The same values apply: kindness, honesty, and good judgement.
</p>

<h2>Your Digital Footprint</h2>
<p>
  Your <span class="keyword" data-def="A digital footprint is the trail of information you leave behind when you use the internet (posts, likes, searches, photos, and hidden metadata).">digital footprint</span>
  is the trail you leave behind online. Some parts are obvious (posts, comments, likes). Other parts are hidden (location data, device information, browsing data).
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Active footprint:</strong> things you choose to share, like posting a photo.</li>
  <li><strong>Passive footprint:</strong> things collected about you, like app trackers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Public vs Private Information</h2>
<p>
  Some information is safe to share publicly. Other information should be kept private or shared only with trusted adults.
</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Public:</strong> hobbies, favourite books, general interests.</li>
  <li><strong>Private:</strong> full name, address, school timetable, passwords, phone number.</li>
  <li><strong>Personal:</strong> anything that can identify you, including photos that show your uniform.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Privacy and Settings</h2>
<p>
  Privacy settings help, but they are not perfect. Always think: <em>Would I be happy if a stranger saw this?</em>
</p>

<h3>Practical rules</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Use strong passwords and never share them.</li>
  <li>Ask for help before joining new platforms or group chats.</li>
  <li>Do not post images that reveal private information in the background.</li>
  <li>Remember: screenshots exist, even if a post is deleted.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Misinformation and Fake News</h2>
<p>
  Not everything online is true. <span class="keyword" data-def="Misinformation is false or misleading information that spreads online.">Misinformation</span>
  can spread quickly because posts are shared before people check them.
</p>

<h3>Check before you share</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Who made it? Are they trustworthy?</li>
  <li>What evidence is shown?</li>
  <li>Is the headline trying to make you angry or shocked?</li>
  <li>Can you confirm the story on another reliable site?</li>
</ul>

<h2>Cyberbullying and Harmful Contact</h2>
<p>
  If something feels wrong, it probably is. <span class="keyword" data-def="Cyberbullying is bullying that happens through messages, posts, group chats, or online games.">Cyberbullying</span>
  can happen in private messages, group chats, or online games.
</p>

<p class="reader-callout">
  <strong>If you need help:</strong> Save evidence, block/report the account, and talk to a trusted adult.
</p>

